WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.960 align:start position:19%
You're falling, feet first, head last,
straight toward the darkest thing in the

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:13.680 align:start position:19%
universe. Below you, a perfect sphere of
nothing, blacker than black, bending the

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:20.000 align:start position:19%
stars around it like a lens. Above you,
the entire universe shrinking.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.120 align:start position:19%
There is no air, no sound, no rescue.
In a few minutes, you will cross a

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.760 align:start position:19%
boundary from which nothing has ever
returned.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.560 align:start position:19%
This is what really happens when you
fall into a black hole.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:35.040 align:start position:19%
Before we begin the fall, one question.
What do you think is inside a black

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:37.280 align:start position:19%
hole? A portal? A crushed star?
Nothing at all?

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.560 align:start position:19%
Drop your answer in the comments,
because by the end of this video, your

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.520 align:start position:19%
answer might change.
Now, let's fall.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.600 align:start position:19%
Right now, you're still far away, where
gravity feels almost normal. But

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.880 align:start position:19%
something strange is already happening
to your body. Your feet closest to the

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.320 align:start position:19%
hole are being pulled harder than your
head.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.520 align:start position:19%
This difference is called a tidal force,
and near a black hole, it becomes a

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:01.120 align:start position:19%
nightmare. Your feet accelerate away
from your head. You stretch first like

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:04.320 align:start position:19%
taffy, then like a thread, then thinner
than any thread. Scientists have a

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.280 align:start position:19%
technical name for this,
spaghettification.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.000 align:start position:19%
It blows my mind that the universe has a
force so extreme it can unravel a human

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.800 align:start position:19%
body atom by atom,
like pulling apart cotton candy.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.400 align:start position:19%
But here's the part that breaks my brain
even more. Time itself is slowing down

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.560 align:start position:19%
for you.
The deeper you fall, the slower your

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.560 align:start position:19%
clock ticks compared to the outside
universe.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:29.200 align:start position:19%
To a distant observer, you appear frozen
at the edge, never quite crossing. But

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.640 align:start position:19%
from your point of view, only seconds
pass,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.880 align:start position:19%
while outside stars are born and die,
galaxies collide. Just think about this

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.920 align:start position:19%
for a second. You could watch the entire
future of the universe fast forward

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.920 align:start position:19%
above you.
And then, you cross it, the event

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.840 align:start position:19%
horizon, the point of no return.
Here's the terrifying part. You wouldn't

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.280 align:start position:19%
feel a thing.
No flash, no barrier, no alarm, just a

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.280 align:start position:19%
quiet, invisible line in space. And the
moment you cross it, every possible

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.640 align:start position:19%
future leads inward. Every road you
could take, every direction you could

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.000 align:start position:19%
turn,
you end at the center.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.960 align:start position:19%
You are now inside the black hole, and
nobody outside will ever see you again.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.759 align:start position:19%
At the center waits the singularity, a
point where all the mass of a dead star

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:15.080 align:start position:19%
is crushed into zero volume.
Density becomes infinite. Our equations

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:19.400 align:start position:19%
of physics simply give up. Einstein's
math breaks. Quantum math breaks.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.680 align:start position:19%
It is the one place in the universe
where the laws of nature, as we

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.520 align:start position:19%
understand them, stop working.
Whatever happens at the singularity is

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.400 align:start position:19%
beyond every theory humans have ever
written.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.760 align:start position:19%
Now, let's pull back because the black
hole you just fell into is small

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.640 align:start position:19%
compared to the real monsters. At the
heart of our own Milky Way sits

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.880 align:start position:19%
Sagittarius A* 4.3 million times the
mass of our sun, 26,000 light years

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.400 align:start position:19%
away.
Every star in our galaxy, including our

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.440 align:start position:19%
sun, is slowly orbiting it. We live in
the suburbs of a sleeping giant, and it

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.320 align:start position:19%
has been quietly sitting there for
billions of years.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:56.360 align:start position:19%
But Ton 618 makes our giant look like a
pebble. This ultra-massive black hole

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.880 align:start position:19%
weighs 66 billion times our sun. So
heavy that its light has traveled 11

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.440 align:start position:19%
billion years to reach us.
When I first read that number, honestly,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:09.840 align:start position:19%
I had to put the article down.
66 billion suns crushed into a single

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:13.800 align:start position:19%
point. The universe doesn't do small.
We're exactly halfway through this fall,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.280 align:start position:19%
and if your brain is melting a little,
good. That means it's working. Quick

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:22.680 align:start position:19%
one. If cosmic mysteries like this are
your thing, hit subscribe. Cosmic

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.480 align:start position:19%
Unknown explores a new one every single
day.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.400 align:start position:19%
Now, back to the fall.
Because here's a twist nobody expected.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:34.640 align:start position:19%
Black holes are not entirely black.
In 1974, Stephen Hawking proved they

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.440 align:start position:19%
slowly
leak.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.080 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; I
am the nobody. Welcome to Cosmic

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.519 align:start position:19%
Unknown.
&gt;&gt; Hawking radiation at the edge of the

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.239 align:start position:19%
event horizon pairs of particles
constantly pop into existence. Sometimes

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:51.200 align:start position:19%
one falls in and the other escapes,
carrying away a tiny bit of the black

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.200 align:start position:19%
black energy.
Over time scales so vast they make the

00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:01.200 align:start position:19%
age of the universe look like a blink, a
black hole evaporates. Completely gone.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.360 align:start position:19%
Which raises a terrifying question.
When it vanishes, where does everything

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:09.280 align:start position:19%
it swallowed go?
That question is called the information

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.720 align:start position:19%
paradox, and it has haunted physics for
50 years.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:17.320 align:start position:19%
Here's why it matters. In quantum
physics, information can never be truly

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:21.200 align:start position:19%
destroyed. Burn a book and the smoke and
ash still contain every word in

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:25.400 align:start position:19%
principle. But a black hole seems to
erase things perfectly.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:30.000 align:start position:19%
My take? I think the answer will rewrite
physics the way Einstein rewrote Newton.

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:34.280 align:start position:19%
And whoever solves it gets a Nobel Prize
on the spot.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.880 align:start position:19%
I remember exactly where I was on April
10th, 2019,

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:42.880 align:start position:19%
when humanity saw one for the first
time. The Event Horizon Telescope, a

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.400 align:start position:19%
virtual telescope the size of planet
Earth, captured the shadow of the black

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.800 align:start position:19%
hole in galaxy M87,
55 million light-years away.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.040 align:start position:19%
A glowing ring of fire around perfect
darkness.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.360 align:start position:19%
I stared at that blurry orange donut for
an hour.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.280 align:start position:19%
After a century of math, we finally had
a photograph.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:06.560 align:start position:19%
And here's what that photo taught us.
Black holes are not cosmic vacuum

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:11.280 align:start position:19%
cleaners randomly eating everything.
They are architects. Almost every large

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.360 align:start position:19%
galaxy has a supermassive black hole at
its center. And the mass of that black

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.400 align:start position:19%
hole decides how many stars the galaxy
can form.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.240 align:start position:19%
They sculpt galaxies the way a heart
drives a body.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.640 align:start position:19%
Without them, the universe would look
completely different.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:30.440 align:start position:19%
We might not exist at all.
So, could anyone survive the fall?

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.240 align:start position:19%
For small black holes, absolutely not.
Spaghettification would kill you long

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.960 align:start position:19%
before the horizon. But for supermassive
ones, the tidal forces at the edge are

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:42.080 align:start position:19%
gentle. You could cross the event
horizon of Sagittarius A* and live for a

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.560 align:start position:19%
while.
Of course, the singularity still waits.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:51.640 align:start position:19%
But physicists have a wild idea. In a
spinning black hole, the math allows a

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:56.880 align:start position:19%
tunnel, a wormhole, straight through.
Probably it collapses instantly.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:59.760 align:start position:19%
Probably. But the math says maybe.
Just think about this for a second.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.160 align:start position:19%
Everything we've described, the
stretching, the frozen time, the

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:06.280 align:start position:19%
evaporating darkness, comes from
equations written by humans sitting at

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.800 align:start position:19%
desks.
Einstein never saw a black hole.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.600 align:start position:19%
Hawking never touched one.
Yet their math predicted all of this

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:17.400 align:start position:19%
decades before any telescope confirmed
it.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:23.240 align:start position:19%
That to me is the most beautiful thing
in science. The universe obeying rules

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:27.240 align:start position:19%
we figured out with nothing but thought.
And here's my honest opinion.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:30.960 align:start position:19%
The deepest mystery isn't what's inside
a black hole.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:34.880 align:start position:19%
It's what black holes are for.
Some physicists now argue that every

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.960 align:start position:19%
black hole might birth a new universe on
the other side of its singularity.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:44.000 align:start position:19%
That our own Big Bang was the inside of
a black hole in some parent cosmos.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:47.160 align:start position:19%
We can't prove it. But it blows my mind
that the darkest objects in existence

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:51.040 align:start position:19%
might be
seeds.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:55.400 align:start position:19%
That destruction and creation could be
the same thing.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:59.280 align:start position:19%
Back to you, falling. Your seconds are
almost up. The light from the outside

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.919 align:start position:19%
universe has red-shifted into darkness.
Your body, stretched across kilometers

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.120 align:start position:19%
of curved space-time, is approaching the
singularity, the place where physics

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.080 align:start position:19%
ends.
For the outside universe, you froze at

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.720 align:start position:19%
the edge forever, a ghost smeared across
the horizon.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.120 align:start position:19%
For you,
there is only the fall.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:20.120 align:start position:19%
And then,
we genuinely don't know.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.320 align:start position:19%
If this journey into the dark gave you
chills, you're in exactly the right

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:29.040 align:start position:19%
place. Subscribe to Cosmic Unknown for a
new cosmic mystery every day. Tomorrow,

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:33.640 align:start position:19%
we chase something even stranger.
And tell me in the comments, would you

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:38.600 align:start position:19%
take the fall if you could survive it?
I read every answer.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.600 align:start position:19%
See you among the stars.

